;; FH-ERROR-001 — containment, and the abandoned candidate that owns nothing.
;;
;; A boundary around a throwing child shows its fallback while a SIBLING
;; subtree keeps rendering — the containment half. And the render that threw
;; published NOTHING: the atomic shell's bundle (F2a) is absent, so the cell
;; is still `:new`, owns no dependencies, and carries no evidence.
{:fh/id "FH-ERROR-001"
 :fh/law
 "An error boundary contains a render-class throw below it — the boundary shows its fallback while every sibling keeps rendering — and the failed candidate publishes nothing at all: the atomic bundle is absent, the cell owns no dependencies and carries no evidence."

 ;; The structural containment scenario. The test mounts a boundary whose
 ;; child throws, beside a sibling that renders normally.
 :fallback [:p.fallback "broken"]
 :sibling  [:aside "sibling"]

 :contained {:boundary-view-id :re-frame.freehand/error-boundary
             :fallback-tag     :p
             :fallback-text    "broken"
             :sibling-tag      :aside
             :sibling-text     "sibling"}

 ;; The abandoned-candidate half: a render body that threw is dropped, so its
 ;; cell publishes none of the F2a bundle.
 :abandoned {:lifecycle        :new
             :dependency-count 0
             :evidence         nil}}
